## Cause

The issue stems from how `this` is bound. In a standard `function`, `this` does not reference the `Timer` object. Instead, when `setInterval` invokes the function, `this` resolves to the global object (in Node.js, this is `globalThis` or the module's wrapper context).

Consequently, `this.seconds` inside the callback attempts to access a `seconds` property on the global object, which does not exist. This property access yields `undefined`. Adding `1` to `undefined` results in `NaN`, and subsequent increments (adding `1` to `NaN`) continue to produce `NaN`.

## Fix

Replace the regular function with an arrow function. Arrow functions lack their own `this` binding—instead, they inherit `this` from the enclosing scope, which in this case is the `Timer` object.

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

With this change, `this` within the callback properly references the `Timer` instance, allowing `this.seconds` to increment correctly by 1 with each interval tick.

